  "Id": 2640,
  "Title": "Literarisierung Reconsidered in the Context of Sultanic Biography: The Case of Shāfiʿ b. ʿAlī's Sīrat al-Nāṣir Muḥammad (BnF MS Arabe 1705)",
  "NewsSubTitle": "By Dr Gowaart Van Den Bossche",
  "NewsSummary": "A new publication by AKU-ISMC's Dr Gowaart Van Den Bossche.",
  "GlobalNews": false,
  "AlternateNewsTitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSubtitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSummary": null,
  "NewsContent": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "hard_break"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "AKU-ISMC's Dr Gowaart Van Den Bossche has recently published a new open access chapter entitled "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Literarisierung Reconsidered in the Context of Sultanic Biography: The Case of Shāfiʿ b. ʿAlī's Sīrat al-Nāṣir Muḥammad (BnF MS Arabe 1705)",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              },
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://brill.com/view/book/edcoll/9789004458901/BP000014.xml",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "in the book "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "New Readings in Arabic Historiography from Late Medieval Egypt and Syria",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              },
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://brill.com/view/title/59478",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ".",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "hard_break"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "While the historiography of late medieval Egypt and Syria is exceptionally well documented and many, if not most, of its major sources have by now been edited and studied, manuscript repositories still contain several historical texts that have received little to no attention from scholars. This chapter presents one such unpublished and mostly unstudied excerpt of a historical biography devoted to Sultan al-Nāṣir Muḥammad (r. 693-4/1293-4, 698-708/1299-1309, 709-41/1309-41) preserved in the manuscript Arabe 1705, held by the Bibliothèque Nationale de France in Paris. Dr Van Den Bossche argues that its author can be identified as Shāfiʿ b. ʿAlī (d. 730/1330), who also wrote two well-known historical biographies of the sultans Baybars (r. 658-76/1260-77) and Qalāwūn (r. 678-89/1281-90). Using this particular text as a case study, he discusses how the concept of \"Literarisierung,\" first applied to Mamluk historiography by Ulrich Haarmann almost half a century ago, may still be used fruitfully to think about how historiography and literary modes of expression interacted. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Read more",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://brill.com/view/book/edcoll/9789004458901/BP000014.xml",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gowaart Van Den Bossche",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "/ismc/people/Pages/gowaart-van-den-bossche.aspx",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Post-Doctoral Research Fellow at AKU-ISMC's "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "KITAB",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "http://kitab-project.org/",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", London. Dr Van Den Bossche studied History and Arabic and Islamic Studies at Ghent University and the Netherlands-Flemish Institute in Cairo. He obtained his PhD in history at Ghent University in January 2019 with a dissertation entitled "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The Past, Panegyric, and the Performance of Penmanship: Sultanic Biography and Social Practice in Late Medieval Egypt and Syria",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". This dissertation won the 2020 BRAIS - De Gruyter Prize in the Study of Islam and the Muslim World. He is generally interested in the narrative qualities of historiography and the ways in which authors made use of literary forms of expression in medieval Islamic written culture."
          },
          {
            "type": "hard_break"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
